Z||000002||batboy||12-01-1999||05:12 PM||||Richard, I thought that the major difference between the auto and the manual tranny V8 swap was the custom flywheel that you need. But, I might be wrong. When I get home this evening, I'll look into this more for you. I've got my brakes (except master cylinder) and the front suspension mods done now, if I can offer any suggestions or advice, don't hesitate to ask.<P>Billfredmike, there is a way to install Olds, Buick, or Pontiac engines into a Fiero using a front wheel drive transaxle from a late 70's to early 80's Buick Riveria or any other GM equivalent model. You lose the Fiero rear trunk and mount the engine longitudinally. These trannies are pretty strong.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||12-02-1999||01:35 PM||||Thanks Batboy. Have you had any problems w/your trans and the V8?  My shifting is so inconsistent presently, one minute it'll be smooth as silk and the next I have to shove with all my might to change gears.  I probably need to revamp the entire clutch system again or mybe it's the linkage binding? I looked at your site. Cool site.  I wish I had enough room to have a couple more Fiero parts cars around. Did you put coil overs on your front end.||||reg||10
Z||000004||batboy||12-02-1999||02:58 PM||||Richard, I have an auto tranny. No problems yet, however, the previous owner trashed the original tranny from hotrodding it too much (he was a former stock car driver).<P>I decided my budget couldn't handle the coil-overs, but they sure would of been nice. I used lowering springs instead. Somewhere is an old thread about all the front end work I did on the Batmobile.<P>I haven't had a chance to look into the flywheel question yet. Have you looked closely at V8 Archie's website? I believe he discusses the auto vs. manual tranny differences when installing a V8 kit.||||reg||1
